492:// tests are about where the rule must NOT apply, which is the whole difficulty:
493:// "the first heading is the title" is true often enough to be dangerous.
494://
495:// AC-18 (2.7's nested-occurrence suppression) IS TESTED, in nested_test.go. Its
496:// first test is the counter-example that corrected the rule from keywords to
497:// spans, and it is first on purpose: the version that reasoned about keywords
498:// passed every other test in that file.
### internal/core/domain/segment/headings.go
1:package segment
2:
3:import (
4:	"github.com/yuin/goldmark/ast"
5:	"github.com/yuin/goldmark/parser"
6:	"github.com/yuin/goldmark/text"
7:	"github.com/yuin/goldmark/util"
8:)
9:
10:// Heading is one ATX heading found in a markdown document.
11://
12:// Every field is a 0-based BYTE offset into the src buffer passed to
13:// detectHeadings, never a rune offset and never a UTF-16 code unit. The offsets
14:// are meaningless against any other buffer; slice the same src that produced
15:// them.
16:type Heading struct {
17:	// Level is the markdown heading level, 1..6, taken from ast.Heading.Level.
18:	// goldmark rejects a run of seven or more '#' outright, so no value outside
19:	// that range can reach this field.
20:	Level int
21:
22:	// ByteStart is the offset of the heading's '#' character (D-07).
23:	ByteStart int
24:
25:	// TextStart is the offset at which the heading text begins, with the '#'
26:	// markers, the whitespace following them, and any closing '###' sequence
27:	// already removed.
28:	TextStart int
29:
30:	// TextStop is exclusive. TextStart == TextStop for an empty heading.
31:	TextStop int
32:
33:	// Inferred marks a heading this package recovered from plain text because
34:	// Mathpix did not emit one (Â§3.1, rule version 2.9). goldmark never sets it;
35:	// only inferReferencesHeading does.
36:	Inferred bool
37:}
38:
39:// mdParser is the block-only markdown parser this package detects headings
40:// with. It is built from an EXPLICIT parser set, and the set below is
41:// exhaustive.
42://
43:// Verified against goldmark v1.8.5 parser/parser.go:462-470, 673-681:
44:// parser.NewConfig initialises BlockParsers to an empty util.PrioritizedSlice
45:// and WithBlockParsers APPENDS to it. DefaultBlockParsers is a separate free
46:// function that NewParser never calls. So a parser built this way contains
47:// exactly the parsers named here and nothing else.
48://
49:// Three properties of this configuration are decisions, not accidents:
50://
51://   - NewSetextHeadingParser is deliberately ABSENT. Because the set is
52://     exhaustive, omitting it genuinely excludes Setext headings from the
53://     grammar. SPLIT-07 is therefore satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by
54://     filtering the output of a parser that ran. With Setext gone and
55://     ThematicBreak present, a --- line under a paragraph becomes a thematic
56://     break and a === line becomes ordinary paragraph continuation text;
57://     neither yields a heading.
58://
59://   - NewParagraphParser is NOT optional, and omitting it is catastrophic
60://     rather than degraded. parseBlocks reads
61://     "if p.openBlocks(...) != newBlocksOpened { return }"
62://     (parser/parser.go:1085-1087), so with no paragraph parser the first
63://     ordinary prose line opens no block, openBlocks returns noBlocksOpened,
64://     and parsing STOPS at that line. Every heading after the first paragraph
65://     would silently disappear with no error of any kind.
66://
67://   - NewListParser, NewListItemParser and NewBlockquoteParser are deliberately
68://     omitted, and dropping them is strictly SAFER for false headings, not
69://     riskier. Without them a blockquoted or list-item heading line becomes a
70://     paragraph line whose first byte is '>' or '-', so the ATX trigger (which
71://     fires on the first non-space byte) never sees a '#'.
72://
73:// Do NOT generalise that last point into "any parser can be dropped safely".
74:// Measured on this configuration during plan 01-04: dropping NewCodeBlockParser
75:// does not merely re-expose an indented '#' as a false heading â€” it makes the
76:// four-space-indented line open no block at all, which ends parseBlocks early
77:// and LOSES every heading after it (3 headings become 1 on the fixture in
78:// TestDetectHeadings_HashInIndentedAndHTMLBlocks). Each of the seven entries
79:// above earns its place; removing one is a behavioural change, not a cleanup.
80://
81:// No inline parsers, no paragraph transformers, no AST transformers and no
82:// renderer are configured: this package needs block structure and byte offsets
83:// only, and never looks inside a heading's inline content.
84:var mdParser = parser.NewParser(
85:	parser.WithBlockParsers(
86:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewThematicBreakParser(), 200), // --- is a rule, never an H2
87:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewCodeBlockParser(), 500),     // 4-space indented code
88:		util.Prioritized(newDisplayMathParser(), 550),          // $$ ... $$ masking (SPLIT-08)
89:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewATXHeadingParser(), 600),
90:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewFencedCodeBlockParser(), 700),
91:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewHTMLBlockParser(), 900),
92:		util.Prioritized(parser.NewParagraphParser(), 1000), // MANDATORY, see above
93:	),
94:)
95:
96:// headingTextRange returns the byte range of a heading's text.
97://
98:// The zero-Lines guard is unconditional on purpose. Three distinct return paths
99:// in atxHeadingParser.Open produce an *ast.Heading carrying ZERO Lines
100:// segments:
101://
102://  1. a bare '#' at EOF with no trailing newline;
103://  2. "## " followed only by whitespace;
104://  3. "### ###", where the whole line is markers.
105://
106:// All three are reachable from OCR output, and on any of them a bare
107:// h.Lines().At(0) is an index-out-of-range panic. An empty heading is reported
108:// as the empty range [Pos, Pos), which keeps TextStart == TextStop true exactly
109:// when the heading has no text.
110:func headingTextRange(h *ast.Heading) (start, stop int) {
111:	if h.Lines().Len() == 0 {
112:		p := h.Pos()
113:		return p, p
114:	}
115:	seg := h.Lines().At(0)
116:	return seg.Start, seg.Stop
117:}
118:
119:// detectHeadings returns every ATX heading in src, in document order.
120://
121:// ByteStart is ast.Node.Pos(), which goldmark sets generically at
122:// parser/parser.go:997 as blockPos.Start + max(pc.BlockOffset(), 0) â€” the
123:// absolute byte offset of the first non-space byte on the block's opening line.
124:// For an ATX heading that byte is the '#'. ast.Heading does not override Pos(),
125:// so it inherits BaseNode.Pos() and that value reaches this function unchanged.
126://
127:// ANTI-PATTERN, stated because it is tempting and wrong: Pos() and
128:// Lines().At(0) are different things BY DESIGN and neither may be derived from
129:// the other. Pos() is the '#' byte. Lines().At(0) is the heading TEXT, with the
130:// markers, the whitespace after them, and any closing '###' sequence already
131:// stripped and the remainder right-space-trimmed. That trimming is why this is
132:// strictly better than a regex splitter: a `^(#{1,6})\\s+(.*)$` capture group
133:// keeps a trailing carriage return on CRLF input, because goldmark's space set
134:// includes 0x0d but a regex `.` does not.
135:// A cross-check that back-scans from Lines().At(0).Start is unsafe for the
136:// three empty-heading cases above; verify Pos() against the raw source bytes
137:// instead, as assertPosIsHashByte does.
138://
139:// INDENTATION SUBTLETY, worth knowing before Phase 4: CommonMark permits an ATX
140:// heading to be indented by up to three spaces. For such a heading Pos() points
141:// at the '#' and NOT at the line start, so under D-08 those leading spaces
142:// belong to the PRECEDING node. Tiling still holds exactly â€” the preceding
143:// node ends where this one begins â€” but md[ByteStart:] does not begin at a line
144:// start for that node.
145://
146:// No sorting happens here and none is needed: goldmark walks the AST in
147:// document order, so the natural collection order already matches the source.
148:func detectHeadings(src []byte) []Heading {
149:	doc := mdParser.Parse(text.NewReader(src))
150:
151:	var out []Heading
152:	_ = ast.Walk(doc, func(n ast.Node, entering bool) (ast.WalkStatus, error) {
153:		if !entering {
154:			return ast.WalkContinue, nil
155:		}
156:		h, ok := n.(*ast.Heading)
157:		if !ok {
158:			return ast.WalkContinue, nil
159:		}
160:		start, stop := headingTextRange(h)
161:		out = append(out, Heading{
162:			Level:     h.Level,
163:			ByteStart: h.Pos(),
164:			TextStart: start,
165:			TextStop:  stop,
166:		})
167:		return ast.WalkContinue, nil
168:	})
169:
170:	return out
171:}
