210:one-line failure message otherwise. Factoring the decision out of the test body is
211:what makes the negative proof possible without touching a single byte of tracked
212:source at run time â€” the alternative, mutating the file and restoring it, is
213:forbidden by this plan's prohibitions.
214:
215:It decides three cases, in this order, and returns exactly one of these messages,
216:each on one line and each naming the fixture file:
217:
218:- When `headings` is empty:
219:  `testdata/demo.md: no headings detected`
220:- When the first heading's `ByteStart` is zero:
221:  `testdata/demo.md: first heading starts at byte 0; AC-14 requires a non-whitespace preamble`
222:- When `strings.TrimSpace` of `src` up to that offset is empty, with N the offset:
223:  `testdata/demo.md: bytes [0,N) are whitespace only`
224:
225:Keep them at that length. Do not name the pinning constants, do not restate the
226:regeneration rationale in each branch, and do not append the offset to the first
227:two â€” the hash and length failures already name exact mismatches, and the
228:comment described below carries the rationale once instead of three times.
229:
230:Extend `TestFixtureIntegrity`, after the existing carriage-return scan, to call
231:`detectHeadings` on the bytes `loadFixture` returned, pass both to
232:`preambleInvariant`, and `t.Fatal` the returned message when it is non-empty. The
233:integrity test must call the function, not reimplement the checks inline â€” if it
234:reimplements them, the control test below exercises a copy and proves nothing
235:about the assertion that actually runs.
236:
237:Do not rely on `TestDetectHeadings_Fixture` having run first. Go gives no ordering
238:guarantee across tests, and this test exists precisely to be the one that fails
239:first.
240:
241:Add `TestPreambleInvariant_Control`, a table test that drives `preambleInvariant`
242:directly with synthetic inputs and is the permanent replacement for the
243:mutation-based negative proof cycle 1 proposed. Four cases:
244:
245:- No headings at all: an empty slice with any source. Expect a non-empty message
246:  containing the fixture filename.
247:- First heading at byte 0. Expect a non-empty message containing the fixture
248:  filename.
249:- A whitespace-only preamble: a source whose leading bytes up to the first
250:  heading's offset are spaces, tabs and newlines. Expect a non-empty message
251:  containing the fixture filename.
252:- A valid preamble: real non-whitespace text before an offset greater than zero.
253:  Expect the empty string.
254:
255:Construct `Heading` values directly rather than parsing markdown; only `ByteStart`
256:matters here and the parser is covered elsewhere. Each violating case must assert
257:the message is non-empty and mentions the file, and the holding case must assert
258:the message is exactly empty â€” that fourth case is what proves the function is not
259:simply returning a message unconditionally.
260:
261:Add `strings` to the import block, in stdlib order.
262:
263:Write one comment above the new block explaining the division of responsibility
264:this change establishes: the property is asserted here, where the fixture is
265:already pinned by hash and length and the answer is therefore decidable from bytes
266:that cannot change without a louder failure firing first; and it is deliberately
267:NOT asserted in the acceptance test, where the same question has to be answered per
268:run and a fixture that legitimately lacked a preamble would have to decline rather
269:than fail. State that this is what keeps the acceptance test's two
270:content-conditional declines green and silent instead of turning them into a gate
271:failure class. State also that a failure here means the fixture was replaced or
272:regenerated, not that the assertion drifted, and that the constants at the top of
273:this file are where to look.
274:
275:Change nothing else in the file. Do not touch `loadFixture`, do not touch the two
276:pinning constants, and do not open the acceptance test for editing.
277:  </action>
